WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.133
Detection logic should be understandable and maintainable.

2
00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:14.727
Clear naming conventions, readable conditions, consistent severity labels, and descriptive metadata help analysts and future maintainers understand what the rule is meant to do.

3
00:00:14.727 --> 00:00:26.221
Documentation should explain the behavior being detected, the data sources required, key assumptions, expected false positives, known limitations, and recommended triage steps.

4
00:00:26.221 --> 00:00:30.212
It should also point to relevant references or internal tickets when useful.

5
00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:33.775
Readable logic matters because detections change over time.

6
00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:43.263
A rule may need tuning after a false positive, a telemetry change, a new business process, a platform migration, or a lesson from an incident.

7
00:00:43.263 --> 00:00:47.394
If nobody can understand the rule, nobody can improve it safely.

8
00:00:47.394 --> 00:00:50.278
Good rule writing also supports collaboration.

9
00:00:50.278 --> 00:01:00.076
Analysts, threat hunters, incident responders, engineers, and managers may all need to understand why a detection exists and how to interpret its output.
